.TH "UMAMI" "1" "2024" "umami 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
umami \(em log anomaly detection and structural tokenization
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]umami\f[R] \f[I]SUBCOMMAND\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
[\f[I]ARGS\f[R]\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]umami\f[R] learns the \f[I]structure\f[R] of streaming log events
and surfaces only the new, changed, and anomalous lines, filtering out
repetitive, already\-seen traffic.
It is a clean\-room reimplementation of the \f[B]unomalycli\f[R] tool
from Unomaly (now Cisco), derived from observable behavior and public
documentation.
It is not affiliated with Unomaly.
.PP
The detection pipeline has four stages:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Tokenize\f[R] \(em split each line into typed tokens (literals,
delimiters, timestamps, IP addresses, quoted strings, numbers, JSON
keys).
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Structure signature\f[R] \(em hash the static tokens (literals and
keys) with FNV\-1a into a stable 64\-bit fingerprint.
Lines that differ only in dynamic values (numbers, timestamps, IPs)
share the same signature.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Profile\f[R] \(em look up the (system, signature) pair in the
in\-memory store.
Track event counts, first/last seen times, per\-position parameter
values (up to 100 distinct values), and inter\-arrival statistics.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Classify\f[R] \(em compare the new event against the learned
profile and emit an anomaly kind, or suppress the event if it matches
normal behavior.
.PP
Anomaly kinds:
.TP
\f[B]never_before_seen\f[R]
The structure has not been seen on any system.
.TP
\f[B]new_in_system\f[R]
The structure has been seen on other systems but not this one.
.TP
\f[B]parameter_change\f[R]
The structure is known but a dynamic parameter has a new value.
.TP
\f[B]frequency_spike\f[R]
Events are arriving significantly faster than the learned baseline
(graded small, medium, or large by z\-score magnitude).
.TP
\f[B]event_stop\f[R]
A periodic structure has gone silent beyond a stop threshold.
.TP
\f[B]system_away\f[R]
A system has stopped sending events entirely.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS tokenize
\f[B]umami tokenize\f[R] [\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enpretty\f[R] \f[I]STYLE\f[R]] [\f[B]\(encolor\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(entest\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R] [\f[B]\(enfix\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enverbose\f[R]]] [\f[I]LINE\f[R]\&...]
.PP
Tokenize one or more log lines and print the result.
If no \f[I]LINE\f[R] arguments are given, lines are read from standard
input.
.TP
\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Select the tokenizer variant.
\f[B]structure\f[R] (default) \(em the modern tokenizer that understands
\f[B]key=value\f[R] pairs (promoting the key to a \f[B]JsonKey\f[R]
token), quoted strings, and JSON key/value contexts.
\f[B]legacy\f[R] \(em the original whitespace\-oriented tokenizer;
splits on whitespace, peels leading/trailing punctuation, and recognizes
IPv4 addresses.
Does not detect ISO 8601 timestamps.
.TP
\f[B]\(enpretty\f[R] \f[I]test\f[R]|\f[I]colors\f[R]|\f[I]params\f[R]|\f[I]delimiters\f[R]|\f[I]static\f[R]
Output format (default: \f[B]test\f[R]).
\f[B]test\f[R] \(em reference format: \f[B]T{text}\f[R] tokens joined by
spaces, where T is the numeric token type (0=literal, 1=delimiter,
2=timestamp, \&...).
\f[B]colors\f[R] \(em original line text with ANSI color highlighting
per token type (use with \f[B]\(encolor\f[R] to activate).
\f[B]params\f[R] \(em original line followed by a
\f[B]\(ha\(ti\(ti\(ti\f[R] marker row marking each dynamic (parameter)
token.
\f[B]delimiters\f[R] \(em original line followed by a \f[B]\(ha\f[R]
marker row marking each delimiter.
\f[B]static\f[R] \(em structural tokens (literals and keys) joined by
spaces; equivalent to \f[B]unomalycli \-pretty=static\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\(encolor\f[R]
Enable ANSI coloring (only meaningful with \f[B]\(enpretty colors\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(entest\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Run a golden test file instead of tokenizing input.
The file format is TSV: \f[I]input\f[R]xpected*, where expected is the
\f[B]test\f[R]\-format output.
Failures are printed to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\(enfix\f[R]
When used with \f[B]\(entest\f[R], rewrite the file with the
tokenizer\(cqs current output, updating any mismatched expected values
in\-place.
.TP
\f[B]\(enverbose\f[R]
With \f[B]\(entest\f[R], also print passing cases.
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
# Tokenize a single line
umami tokenize \(dqJan 23 10:23:45 web01 sshd[1234]: ok\(dq

# Show dynamic\-parameter positions
umami tokenize \-\-pretty params \(dqlevel=info duration=3.14 status=ok\(dq

# Colorized output
umami tokenize \-\-pretty colors \-\-color \(dqdisk full on /dev/sda1\(dq

# Read from stdin; show only structural tokens
journalctl \-n 500 | umami tokenize \-\-pretty static

# Check a corpus file
umami tokenize \-\-test corpus/structure.tsv
.EE
.SS pipeline
\f[B]umami pipeline\f[R] [\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]] [\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enshow\-all\f[R]]
.PP
Read log lines from standard input, run each through the detector, and
print anomalous lines to standard output.
Each output line is prefixed with the anomaly kind padded to 18
characters, then the original line.
.PP
Without \f[B]\(enshow\-all\f[R], normal (non\-anomalous) lines are
suppressed.
.TP
\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Attribute events to this system name (default: \f[B]stdin\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]
Also emit \f[B]parameter_change\f[R] anomalies when a known structure
receives a previously\-unseen parameter value.
.TP
\f[B]\(enshow\-all\f[R]
Print every line; prefix normal lines with \f[B]ok\f[R] instead of
suppressing them.
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami pipeline \-\-system web01

# Show all lines annotated
cat syslog | umami pipeline \-\-show\-all

# Detect parameter changes too
cat access.log | umami pipeline \-\-parameters
.EE
.SS sim
\f[B]umami sim\f[R] [\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]] [\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]]
.PP
Consume all of standard input and print a summary of anomaly counts,
total event count, and the noise\-reduction percentage (fraction of
events filtered as normal).
.TP
\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
System name (default: \f[B]stdin\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]
Include parameter\-change anomalies in the count.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami sim \-\-system prod\-web01

events:    10000
anomalies: 47
reduction: 99.5%
profiles:  312
  never_before_seen  31
  new_in_system      12
  parameter_change    4
.EE
.SS ingest
\f[B]umami ingest\f[R] [\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(entagged\f[R]] [\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]]
.PP
Read log lines from standard input and emit one JSON object per
anomalous event to standard output (newline\-delimited JSON).
Each JSON object has the fields: \f[B]system\f[R], \f[B]kind\f[R],
\f[B]score\f[R] (float 0\(en1), \f[B]signature\f[R] (structural tokens),
\f[B]line\f[R] (original log line).
.TP
\f[B]\(ensystem\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Default system name when input is not tagged (default: \f[B]stdin\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(entagged\f[R]
Treat each input line as \f[I]system\f[R]ogline*.
This allows multiple systems to be multiplexed on one input stream.
Lines without a tab are attributed to \f[B]\(ensystem\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(enparameters\f[R]
Emit parameter\-change events.
.SS Examples
.IP
.EX
cat /var/log/syslog | umami ingest \-\-system web01 | jq .

# Multi\-system input
paste \-d\(aq\(rst\(aq systems.txt lines.txt | umami ingest \-\-tagged

# Example output line
{\(dqsystem\(dq:\(dqweb01\(dq,\(dqkind\(dq:\(dqnever_before_seen\(dq,\(dqscore\(dq:1.00,
 \(dqsignature\(dq:\(dqdisk full on\(dq,\(dqline\(dq:\(dqdisk full on /dev/sda1\(dq}
.EE
.SS load
\f[B]umami load\f[R] [\f[B]\(encount\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]]
[\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]] [\f[B]\(enstdin\f[R]]
.PP
Throughput benchmark.
By default generates \f[I]N\f[R] synthetic log lines and measures how
fast the detector processes them.
With \f[B]\(enstdin\f[R], reads from standard input instead.
.TP
\f[B]\(encount\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Number of synthetic events to generate (default: \f[B]1000000\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(entokenizer\f[R] \f[I]structure\f[R]|\f[I]legacy\f[R]
Tokenizer variant (default: \f[B]structure\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\(enstdin\f[R]
Read lines from standard input instead of generating synthetic data.
.SS Example
.IP
.EX
umami load
umami load \-\-count 500000
cat big.log | umami load \-\-stdin
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
An error occurred (bad argument, I/O error, test failure).
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]corpus/structure.tsv\f[R], \f[B]corpus/legacy.tsv\f[R]
Golden test corpora in \f[I]input\f[R]xpected* TSV format, captured from
the reference \f[B]unomalycli\f[R] binary and used by the integration
tests.
